---
title: Modules and plugins
description: IModule is the extension point the Command, Query and Event modules themselves use — and the way to add your own, inside a closed composition table.
---

Ergosfare's core knows about messages, handlers, interceptors and pipeline plans. It knows
nothing about *commands*, *queries* or *events*. Those three are **modules** built on the same
extension point you can use yourself.

That is not a metaphor. `AddCommandModule` is four lines:

```csharp
public static IModuleRegistry AddCommandModule(
    this IModuleRegistry moduleRegistry,
    Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builderAction)
{
    moduleRegistry.Register(new CommandModule(builderAction));
    return moduleRegistry;
}
```

## The contract

A module is anything implementing `IModule`:

```csharp
public interface IModule
{
    void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration);
}
```

`Build` runs once, at registration time. It receives everything a module needs:

```csharp
public interface IModuleConfiguration
{
    IServiceCollection Services { get; }             // register your own services
    FrozenCompositionCatalog Compositions { get; }   // select from the compiled table
}
```

Here is the whole command module:

```csharp
internal class CommandModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<CommandModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public CommandModule(Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new CommandModuleBuilder(configuration.Compositions));

        configuration.Services.TryAddTransient<ICommandMediator, EngineBackedCommandMediator>();
    }
}
```

Two responsibilities: run the user's registration lambda against a builder, and add the
module's own facade to DI. `TryAdd` rather than `Add`, so registering a module twice is
harmless.

**Transient, not scoped.** A mediator facade is stateless — the only thing it captures is the
provider that resolved it, and a transient service is handed the resolving scope's provider
just as a scoped one is, so handlers still resolve from the calling scope. Registering it
scoped would take the scope's lock and write the instance into the scope's resolved-services
dictionary on every dispatch that creates its own scope, with nothing to amortize it. Follow
this in your own module. See [Performance](/ergosfare.docs/preview/advanced/performance#mediator-lifetime).

## A closed world

`Compositions` is a **selection surface, not a registry**. The frozen composition table is
compiled for the whole closure before the application runs; a module chooses which of its
participant rows this container serves. It cannot append a row.

That is the constraint a custom module has to design around, and it is deliberate: if a
module could add a message shape at runtime, dispatch would need a registration window, a
version check and an invalidation path — the three things v2.3 removed.

The practical consequence: **a new message shape must bind its constructs to one of the
built-in markers** so the generator partitions them at compile time. Tagging is how you do
that.

## Writing your own module

The case for a custom module is a *new message shape* — something you want to name, route and
observe separately, but which runs on the same pipeline, the same interceptor stages and the
same compile-time discovery.

```csharp
public sealed class SagaModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<SagaModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public SagaModule(Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new SagaModuleBuilder(configuration.Compositions));

        configuration.Services.TryAddTransient<ISagaMediator, SagaMediator>();
    }
}

public static class SagaModuleRegistryExtensions
{
    public static IModuleRegistry AddSagaModule(
        this IModuleRegistry registry, Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builderAction)
    {
        registry.Register(new SagaModule(builderAction));
        return registry;
    }
}
```

Your builder wraps the same catalog the built-in builders wrap:

```csharp
public sealed class SagaModuleBuilder(FrozenCompositionCatalog compositions)
{
    public SagaModuleBuilder Register<T>() where T : ISaga
    {
        compositions.Select(typeof(T));
        return this;
    }
}
```

And the saga contracts carry a built-in marker, which is what puts them in the generator's
partitions:

```csharp
public interface ISaga : IEvent;                       // rides the event partition

public interface ISagaHandler<in TSaga> : ISaga, IAsyncHandler<TSaga>
    where TSaga : ISaga;
```

Used exactly like the built-in modules:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddSagaModule(s => s.Register<OrderSaga>()));
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
The source generator emits `RegisterGenerated()` only for the three built-in module builders.
A custom module selects with explicit `Register<T>()` calls, or exposes its own bulk method
over `FrozenCompositionCatalog.Select(IEnumerable<Type>)`. There is no assembly-scanning
fallback — `RegisterFromAssembly` and `RegisterDescriptors` were removed in v2.3.
{% /aside %}

## Module builders

There is no `IModuleBuilder` interface to implement. Each built-in builder is a sealed class
over the catalog, exposing the same three entry points by convention:

```csharp
public sealed class CommandModuleBuilder(FrozenCompositionCatalog compositions)
{
    public CommandModuleBuilder Register<T>() where T : ICommand;
    public CommandModuleBuilder Register(Type type);
    public CommandModuleBuilder RegisterParticipants(IEnumerable<Type> participantTypes);
}
```

`Register` asserts module membership — a query handler passed to `CommandModuleBuilder`
throws — while `RegisterParticipants` is the unchecked bulk path generated registration uses,
because the generator has already partitioned its discoveries by module.

## Plain messages do not need a module

Before writing one, check whether the event module already covers you. `IEventHandler<TEvent>`
has **no constraint on `TEvent`**, and `PublishAsync<TEvent>` accepts any non-null type:

```csharp
public interface IEventHandler<in TEvent> : IEvent, IAsyncHandler<TEvent>
    where TEvent : notnull;
```

So a POCO — a class implementing nothing, a `record struct`, a message type from a wire
contract you do not own — is publishable as-is. That is the open lane, and it is where the
removed `AddCoreModule` traffic went.

## Only take what you need

Because the modules are independent, an application that never publishes events never
registers the event module, never resolves `IEventMediator`, and never carries its assembly:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated()));   // that's it
```

The same applies at the package level — see
[Installation](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/installation).

Next: [Modules and extensions](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-components/modules-extensions) walks
through the shipped modules in detail.
